## Executive Summary

The core thesis is compelling: LTS handled 2,812,195 metric tons (MT) in FY2025 on $55.6M in revenue, averaging approximately $19.8/MT [1][2]. With all four loaders and three stackers at 100% utilization [3], production rate improvement — not fleet expansion — is the only near-term lever to grow revenue. A validated 3–5% production rate improvement could yield an incremental 84K–141K MT, translating to **$1.44M–$2.38M in additional revenue** and an estimated **$0.638M–$1.1M in EBITDA contribution** on the FY2026 budgeted EBITDA base of $39.5M [4]. This represents a 1.6–2.8% EBITDA uplift with an estimated implementation cost well under $212K given the pre-existing hardware footprint.

## Market Context

### Industry Overview

The largest publicly traded U.S. terminal handling contractor, Aldermont Bulk Group (ABG), reported 2024 revenues of $648M with a gross margin of 21.1% and Adjusted EBITDA of $116M [15]. LTS's FY2025 gross margin of 51.3% and EBITDA margin of 43.6% [1] dramatically outperform this benchmark, reflecting LTS's maintenance-focused business model with margins ranging from 55–90% on maintenance work versus 30–50% on new build projects [16].

